{"cases":[{"target_alias":"SRC_001","attack":true,"case":"The target's claim conflates two incompatible geometric regimes. It asserts the answer is 8 because all four cuts pass through the exact center (concurrent lines), yet simultaneously invokes physical cut-thickness as part of its reasoning. These cannot coherently combine: if the cuts are idealized as concurrent through a single point, thickness is irrelevant and the answer is 8; if the cuts are physical with width, the central point of concurrence is destroyed, and the argument that the count is 'still 8' is asserted without proof — thick cuts through a destroyed center could just as well yield the standard 11-piece arrangement of four non-concurrent lines. The target thus fails the stated problem by grounding its answer in an incoherent hybrid of idealized and physical models rather than cleanly applying the concurrent-lines partition that the problem stipulates.","counterexample":null},{"target_alias":"SRC_002","attack":false,"case":"","counterexample":null}]}